{
  // CI dependency-vulnerability gate (replaces raw `npm audit --audit-level=high`).
  // Fails the build on any high/critical advisory EXCEPT the reviewed, time-boxed
  // exceptions in `allowlist` below. New/unexpected highs still break CI.
  //
  // Run locally with: npm run audit
  "high": true,
  "critical": true,
  "report-type": "important",
  "allowlist": [
    // GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2 — react-router "RSC Mode CSRF Bypass".
    // NOT EXPLOITABLE HERE: this is a client-only Vite SPA that does not use
    // React Router's RSC/server mode (no createStaticHandler, no server data
    // routers) — the vulnerable code path is never mounted.
    // NO FIX AVAILABLE: as of 2026-07-25 the advisory's vulnerable range is
    // react-router 7.12.0–8.2.0 and 7.18.1 (our version) is the latest published
    // release, so the only audit-clean bump is a downgrade to 7.11.0. We are NOT
    // downgrading the router for a non-applicable advisory.
    // REVISIT — remove this entry (and re-evaluate as a real risk) on EITHER
    // trigger, whichever comes first:
    //   1. react-router ships a patched release above the vulnerable range —
    //      then bump react-router-dom to it and drop this entry.
    //   2. this app stops being a static client-only SPA — i.e. it adds React
    //      Router RSC/server mode, ANY route `action`/`loader` that mutates, or
    //      an authenticated/mutating server endpoint. That is the change that
    //      makes this advisory actually exploitable, so it must re-open the
    //      review immediately, NOT wait for a patched release.
    "GHSA-qwww-vcr4-c8h2"

    // GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (brace-expansion DoS) WAS allowlisted here and was
    // REMOVED 2026-08-03, because the entry had quietly stopped being true.
    //
    // What it claimed: that the `overrides` pinned brace-expansion to patched
    // versions (1.1.16 / 5.0.8) and npm audit was raising a FALSE POSITIVE
    // because it flattened the advisory's non-contiguous patched ranges into a
    // single `<=5.0.7` and could not represent the backported 1.x fix.
    //
    // That was accurate when written. It stopped being accurate when the
    // advisory was updated to carry an explicit 1.x range of `<1.1.17`: at that
    // point 1.1.16 was genuinely vulnerable, npm audit was reporting it
    // CORRECTLY, and this entry was suppressing a real high-severity finding
    // rather than a false positive. Nothing failed, which is the problem — the
    // gate stayed green across that change with no signal that the reasoning
    // underneath it had expired.
    //
    // Fixed properly instead of re-allowlisted: the overrides now pin
    // `brace-expansion@1` to ^1.1.18 and `brace-expansion@5` to ^5.0.9, which
    // clears BOTH GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg (<1.1.17) and the newer
    // GHSA-rgw5-rvv9-x895 (<1.1.18 on the 1.x line, >=4.0.0 <5.0.9 on 5.x).
    // The 1.x/5.x split is still required — brace-expansion 5.x is ESM-first
    // and crashes eslint's legacy minimatch 3.x — so the two-line override
    // stays; only the versions moved. `npm run audit` now passes with this
    // entry absent, and audit-ci itself reports "Consider not allowlisting
    // advisory: GHSA-mh99-v99m-4gvg".
    //
    // STANDING LESSON for anything added above: an allowlist entry justified by
    // "the installed version is already patched" is only true against the
    // advisory's range AS IT READS TODAY. Advisory ranges get widened. Such an
    // entry must be re-checked against the live range, not trusted because it
    // was reviewed once.
  ]
}
